WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.067 --> 00:00:03.630
(rocket booming)

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.220
- [Narrator] North Korea
has unveiled a host

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.963
of new weapons in recent weeks.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.200
They include a tactical nuclear warhead,

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.299
an underwater drone

00:00:11.299 --> 00:00:12.660
(camera clicking)

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.484
and a solid fueled
intercontinental ballistic missile.

00:00:15.484 --> 00:00:17.790
(cameras clicking)

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:18.960
The significant advances

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.150
in Pyongyang's arsenal were a major focus

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:22.800
of President Biden's latest talks

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.990
with his South Korean counterpart.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.680
- Our mutual defense treaty is ironclad

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:33.180
and that includes our commitment
to extended deterrents

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.590
and that includes the nuclear threat

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.735
and the nuclear deterrent.

00:00:39.735 --> 00:00:40.568
- [Narrator] We look at what Pyongyang

00:00:40.568 --> 00:00:42.690
said their newest weapons are capable of,

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:45.359
and how they add to the growing
threat from North Korea.

00:00:45.359 --> 00:00:47.450
(rocket whooshing)

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:48.390
(pensive music)

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.430
In late March, North Korea leader

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:52.080
Kim Jong Un ordered the expansion

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.700
of weapons grade nuclear material

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.830
to boost the country's arsenal.

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:56.700
That same week,

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.820
state media released photos
showing Kim inspecting weapons

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.060
including what North
Korea calls the Hwasan-31.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.220
It was the first time
Pyongyang provided evidence

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.070
to back its claim that it
has tactical nuclear warheads

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:09.920
but could be mounted on its missiles.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.450
By the beginning of April,

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:15.300
Pyongyang said an underwater
drone it tested twice

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.910
is capable of conducting nuclear attacks.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.370
Referring to an earlier
exercise, state media

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.997
said the drone could trigger a
quote, "Radioactive tsunami."

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:28.140
(dramatic music)

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.259
On April 13th, in another first,

00:01:30.259 --> 00:01:32.010
(head popping)

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:33.810
Pyongyang launched a solid fueled

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.622
intercontinental ballistic missile.

00:01:35.622 --> 00:01:38.610
(missile booming)

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.790
Experts said if this solid
fuel technology is perfected,

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:43.920
the long range weapon
can be deployed faster

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.870
and is harder to detect.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.060
The missile traveled
for more than 600 miles

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.180
before landing in waters
east of the Korean peninsula.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.120
The US and the UN condemned the test.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.580
- The Secretary General
reiterates his call

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.830
on the Democratic
People's Republic of Korea

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:02.100
to fully comply with its
international obligations

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.460
under all relevant Security
Council resolutions

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.860
to reopen communications channels

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.960
and to resume dialogue leading

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.120
to sustainable peace and the complete

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.704
and verifiable denuclearization
of the Korean peninsula.

00:02:16.704 --> 00:02:18.210
(tanks whooshing)

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:19.680
- [Narrator] North Korea
said the launch was part

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.230
of its response to consecutive
joint military drills

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.586
between the US and South Korea.

00:02:24.586 --> 00:02:26.290
(soldiers shouting)

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:27.990
(pensive music)

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:29.610
Less than a week after the launch,

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:31.050
Pyongyang said it finished building

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.030
the country's first spy satellite,

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.130
which is aimed at collecting
real-time information

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.083
on the US military and its allies.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.930
Shortly after state media released photos

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:42.900
of Kim and his daughter visiting
the country's space agency,

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:43.800
he ordered the launch

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.100
of the satellite without
giving an exact date.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.350
- Welcome to the White House.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.660
- [Narrator] On April 26th,
South Korean president,

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.750
Yoon Suk-yeol, met with
President Biden in the US.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.000
Both leaders vowed to
take overwhelming action

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.490
if North Korea launches a nuclear attack.

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:01.710
The allies will set up a
group to share information

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:03.600
on nuclear operations and planning

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.700
and the US will send strategic assets

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.836
to South Korea regularly.

00:03:07.836 --> 00:03:10.669
(missile booming)

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.200
Experts said the latest spree

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.540
of missile launches and
display of new technology

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.450
show North Korea's weapons
program has made rapid gains

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:21.144
and the advances are expected to continue.

00:03:21.144 --> 00:03:23.894
(dramatic music)

